  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx\\,dy=$",
  "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x-\\frac{y{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{{y}^{2}}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
